<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Donghu</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Eastern Hu nomads of Inner Mongolia and Manchuria, crushed by the Xiongnu leader Modu around 206 BCE; their survivors became the Wuhuan and Xianbei.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chinese sources use Donghu, the Eastern Hu, for pastoral peoples living east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> from about the 7th century BCE. Archaeologists often link them with the Upper Xiajiadian culture, though the connection is debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were a confederation of herding tribes with cavalry strong enough to raid the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yan">State of Yan</a>, until the Yan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Kai drove them back around 300 BCE and Yan built walls against them. Nothing is known of their language or rulers' names.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, the Donghu demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu's</a> best horse, then one of his wives, then land; Modu granted the first two and answered the third by destroying them. The survivors fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wuhuan">Wuhuan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> mountains and gave their names to the Wuhuan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="nomadic confederation, 700 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">nomadic confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:9.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Hu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-453&amp;focus=donghu" aria-label="Donghu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7407 1009 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Donghu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7727 1265h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7727 1265h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-453&amp;focus=donghu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

